const title = 'How to get an apostille in Florida';
const description = 'Florida apostille and notarial certification: the official $10 or $20 fee, Tallahassee walk-in service, mail addresses, and current processing dates from the Division of Corporations.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-22">
  <h1>How to get an apostille in Florida</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Florida Secretary of State is the only competent authority authorized to issue notarial and apostille certifications in Florida. The Division of Corporations issues them from its Tallahassee office.</p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>What the official page says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Issuing office</td><td>Florida Department of State, Division of Corporations, Apostille Section, The Centre of Tallahassee, 2415 N. Monroe Street, Suite 810, Tallahassee, FL 32303<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fee</td><td>$10.00 per document; or $20.00 per document for documents certified by any Florida county clerk of court when you are requesting an apostille ($10 for the apostille and $10 for a certificate of incumbency).<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Payment</td><td>Check or money order payable to the Florida Department of State, in U.S. currency drawn from a U.S. bank. Cash and credit cards are not accepted.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail</td><td>Yes. Include the official request form, the document, payment, and a self-addressed stamped envelope or a prepaid, pre-addressed air bill with the requestor’s name and address as both sender and recipient.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mailing addresses</td><td>Mail: Department of State, Division of Corporations, Apostilles Section, P.O. Box 6800, Tallahassee, FL 32314 (the official request form prints the ZIP as 32314-6800). Courier or physical: 2415 N. Monroe Street, Suite 810, Tallahassee, FL 32303.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/contact/address-phone-numbers/" /><Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/media/702388/1-apostille_and_notarial_certificate_request_form-10-19-2023.pdf" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In person</td><td>Walk-in service at 2415 N. Monroe Street, Suite 810, Tallahassee, Monday through Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., except state holidays. “Most requests may be completed while you wait.” The office does not offer expedited service.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification/faq" /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing as published</td><td>Documents are processed in the order received. The document-processing-dates page, updated November 20, 2026, listed apostille requests as currently processing documents received November 16, 2026.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/document-processing-dates" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to file</h2>
  <p>The official “How to File” steps are:<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></p>
  <ol>
    <li>Complete the Department of State’s Apostille and Notarial Certificate Request Form.</li>
    <li>Enclose the document. An original certified copy or certificate of status of a public document is required; a photocopy is not acceptable. Notarized documents must contain a full notarial statement and the original notary’s signature, stamp, and seal.</li>
    <li>Insert a self-addressed stamped envelope, or a prepaid, pre-addressed air bill, with the requestor listed as both sender and recipient.</li>
    <li>Include payment.</li>
    <li>Mail the packet to the Apostille Section.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Apostille or notarial certification</h2>
  <p>An apostille is issued for use in a country that is a member of the 1961 Hague Convention. “Countries that are nonmembers of the 1961 Hague Convention are not issued an apostille; they are issued notarial certificates or certifications.” Both certificates authenticate the issuing official’s or notary’s signature.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification/faq" /></p>

  <h2>Which documents the office lists</h2>
  <p>The official page lists, among others: Florida agency or official documents; Florida birth and death certificates; circuit court and clerk-of-the-county documents; marriage and divorce certificates; school transcripts; vehicle titles; documents notarized by a Florida notary public; Florida criminal background checks from the Florida Department of Law Enforcement or local law enforcement; and Secretary of State certified copies and certificates of status.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></p>
  <p>Federal documents should be submitted to the U.S. Department of State. A document issued or notarized in another state should go to that state’s competent authority.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification" /></p>
  <p>The office does not translate documents. It also states that the person who translates a document cannot be the person who notarizes it.<Src href="https://dos.fl.gov/sunbiz/other-services/apostille-notarial-certification/faq" /></p>

